          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Chris:</strong> Conclusions from the use of coding principles and practices</p>
<p>Following coding principles will help developer teams to reduce dependencies, and make designs easier to understand, maintain and extend. This helps developers to build adaptive, effective software which would also be in line with Agile methodology.</p>
<p>However, following coding principles can extend the design process and make development more difficult, and it can be seen as a tedious task by some developers.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> As the intention of the principles is to make code more readable, easier to understand, easier to test and maintain; most developer teams will consider the effort and extra time to implement them a valuable exercise, as this will help to avoid issues that could occur from not following them at a later date.</p>
<p>So let’s look at some of the principles we have already discussed and how they can affect code starting with KISS.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> As we discussed in the previous video the main rule with KISS is that the code should be simple and therefore easy to modify. By keeping the programming simple, service and maintenance are much easier, problems are solved quicker, the code is deliberately concise, and it is easier to understand, extend, change, and reuse.</p>
<p>Now let’s consider SOLID</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> S - The Single Responsibility Principle [SRP] title on screen</p>
<p>Good practice of SRP makes code easier to test and maintain and will prevent side-effects from future changes, making the software easier to implement.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> O – Open / Closed Principle [OCP] title on screen</p>
<p>Good practice of OCP makes code that is easy to maintain and revise as you can add new functionality without changing the existing code. This is useful in agile methodologies as stakeholders’ requirements are likely to change.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> L – Liskov Substitution Principle [LSP] title on screen</p>
<p>Good practice of LSP helps to avoid unexpected consequences of changes, avoids having to open a closed class, avoids issues that could occur during updates and extensions, and allows easy extensions of software.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> I – Interface Segregation Principle [ISP] title on screen</p>
<p>With ISP as the developers would be focused on their own client specific interface, they are not forced to depend on interfaces that they do not use. This would save the developer time and increase their motivation and focus.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> D – Dependency Inversion Principle [DIP] title on screen</p>
<p>As high-level modules [which sustain complex systems] are not affected by low-level modules [which provide utility features] with DIP; this premise would speed up development and make it easier to develop and update the code.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> So, in conclusion using good coding practices and principles can help create better and more organised workflow, increased efficiency and productivity, enhanced product quality and subsequently reduced costs. Also using coding practices and principles to avoid the frustration that comes with bugs and errors should be motivation enough in itself.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
